[interviewer · CLARIFY · 1:33] On what you asked for: 200 stores today — and it was 200 stores two years ago, so the footprint hasn’t changed. On the data you asked for — I'll come to that shortly. Is that structure MECE, and what's missing from it? Also, which branch would you prioritize first, and why?

[candidate · 3:03] Good — that actually sharpens things. If the store count is flat, the 15% revenue growth is all same-store, so it's either price, traffic, or ticket size. That matters because if they raised prices and revenue only grew 15%, traffic may have fallen.

On MECE: my structure holds at the top, but I was sloppy below it. I lumped marketing in with overhead when it's a discretionary spend that behaves differently from rent. I also left out things that sit below operating profit — interest expense, D&A, taxes — and "net profit margin" includes those. If they took on debt or did a big remodel, depreciation alone could explain part of this.

I'd prioritize costs. Revenue grew, so the top line isn't the problem; something is eating the growth. And an 18-point margin drop in two years is too fast for anything structural on the demand side.

I'd want the cost lines as a percent of revenue, both years, to see which one widened.
