[interviewer · ANALYSIS · 3:07] COGS is 58% of revenue today, up from 42% two years ago. Labor is 22% of revenue, and it’s been stable. Overhead is 14% of revenue, up slightly from 12%. Based on those figures, which line would you dig into first?

[candidate · 4:37] Let me check the arithmetic. Today: 100 minus 58 minus 22 minus 14 leaves 6%. Two years ago: 100 minus 42 minus 22 minus 12 leaves 24%. So the lines fully reconcile to the margin — nothing hiding below the operating line after all.

COGS moved 16 points of the 18. Overhead is 2 points and labor is flat, so that's where I'm going.

Now, COGS as a percent of revenue can rise two ways: input costs went up, or prices didn't keep pace. The 15% revenue growth could easily be volume alone with flat menu prices, in which case they've absorbed every bit of input inflation. I'll take it that prices held flat unless told otherwise.

Inside COGS I'd want the split — green coffee, dairy, cups and packaging, food — because a 16-point move usually traces to one dominant input, and coffee beans are the obvious candidate. Spoilage and waste would be the other thing I'd look at.
