[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 4:39] The arithmetic reconciles, and the cost lines are all revealed now. On what you asked for: Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. You said you'd take it that prices held flat unless told otherwise, so what data would you want to confirm or reject that?

[candidate · 6:09] Let me do the decomposition first. Old COGS was 42 points of revenue; beans at 25% of that is 10.5 points, the other inputs 31.5 points. Those others up 37.5% takes them to roughly 43.3 points. Add beans unchanged and you'd be at about 53.8, but actual COGS is 58 — so beans are carrying roughly 14.7 points, up about 40% themselves. So it's broad input inflation, both buckets, not one rogue commodity.

Though that only holds if these are percent-of-revenue moves and not just absolute cost inflation against a growing base — so I'd want to be careful there.

On the pricing assumption: the clean test is the menu price history, item by item, over the two years. If average menu price is flat or near-flat, that confirms it. The corroborating piece is average ticket versus transaction count — if revenue grew 15% purely on transactions with flat ticket, prices definitely didn't move.
