[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 6:13] I'm not asserting their math either way. Menu prices have not changed in two years. The average transaction is $6.80, up from $6.20 two years ago — about a 10% increase, because customers are buying more items per visit.

[candidate · 7:43] That's a clean story now. Revenue up 15% with ticket up 10% means transactions grew only about 4.5% — 1.15 divided by 1.10 is roughly 1.045. So the top-line growth is mostly basket size, not traffic, and none of it is price.

So the root cause: input costs rose roughly 40% across the board and Brew & Bean passed through zero of it. Labor held, overhead drifted 2 points, but the 16-point COGS move is almost entirely a pricing failure, not a cost-management failure.

Quick sizing on the fix. Hold costs absolute and raise prices x%. COGS falls to 58/(1+x), labor 22/(1+x), overhead 14/(1+x). At x = 15%, total costs go to 94/1.15 ≈ 81.7, so margin ≈ 18%. At 10%, margin ≈ 14.5%.

What I'd want before committing is elasticity — do we have anything on how traffic responded to price in comparable chains, or by location?
