[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 7:49] You haven't asked for bean price data yet, so I'll hold that. Your sizing treats labor and overhead as fixed in absolute dollars while prices rise. Beyond a price increase, what else could Brew & Bean do to restore margin?

[candidate · 9:19] Fair push on the assumption — labor especially isn't fixed. If more items per visit means more drinks to make, labor hours scale with volume, not with price, so holding it flat in dollars is defensible for a pure price move but not if traffic shifts. Overhead is the more genuinely fixed line. So treat my 18% as the optimistic end.

Other levers, roughly in order of impact:

Procurement. A 40% move on beans suggests they're buying spot or on short contracts. Forward contracts or hedging, and renegotiating with 200 stores of scale, should claw back some of that.

Mix. Ticket is up because people buy more items — if we know which items carry the best margin, we push those. Food and specialty drinks usually beat drip coffee.

Portion and waste control. Milk and food spoil; a 16-point COGS move can hide real shrink.

Then product reformulation — cup sizes, dairy alternatives sourcing.

I'd still want the bean cost trend itself, since whether that's a market move or a bad contract changes which lever leads.
