[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 9:22] Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years.

[candidate · 10:52] That matches the number I backed into — I had beans implied at roughly 40%, so the decomposition holds and both buckets inflated at about the same rate.

That changes the emphasis. A 40% market move in green coffee isn't a procurement failure; it's a commodity cycle hitting every specialty chain in the country. So renegotiation gets us a few points at best, not sixteen. And critically, competitors are absorbing the same shock — which makes a price increase much safer, because they're almost certainly raising too. Brew & Bean holding menu prices flat for two years while the whole category inflates is the anomaly here.

It also means hedging is a forward-looking fix, not a recovery lever. It protects the next cycle; it doesn't recover the margin already lost.

The thing I'd still want is whether bean costs are expected to stay elevated or mean-revert, since that decides whether we price permanently or ride it out.
